An observa-  tion of interest made by Mr. Sana Ullah is that the deterioration of copper or  bronze, to which such antiquities are liable when exposed to the atmosphere,  is not necessarily due to the presence of soluble chlorides within the object,  since, in several case.” this ‘ disease ’ has been seriously developed even after  prolonged washing vdth distilled water. The cau.se in such cases, he finds,  lies in the presence of insoluble cuprous chloride, which changes into ataca-  niite under the influence of oxygen and water, which are present m the atmos-  phere. Cupric chloride, which is liberated simultaneously during the reaction,  attacks cuprous oxide oi’ copper, forming more atacamite. It is, therefore,  obvious that to ensure proper preservation against this ‘ disease ’ it is essential  to destroy all traces of cuprous chloride that ma)’- be present in the objects.  This can be accomplished by treatment with solutions of caustic soda, potash,  or potassium cyanide. In our laboratory all copper or bronze antiquities after  necessary cleaning are left in a 10 per cent, solution of caustic soda foi* at  least 12 hours, before final -washing and impregnation. This precaution is  not necessary in the case of those objects which have undergone a reduction  or alkaline tartarate - treatments. 

