Research Article Inequality declined in the Bronze Age city of Mohenjo-daro Adam S. Green 1,2 , Iqtedar Alam 3 & Cameron Petrie 3 1 Department of Archaeology, University of York, UK 2 Department of Environment and Geography, University of York, UK 3 Department of Archaeology, University of Cambridge, UK Author for correspondence: Adam S. Green ✉ adam.green@york.ac.uk Mohenjo-daro was a major city of the Indus Civilisation ( c.2 6 0 0– 1900 BC), with excavations revealing evidence for public infrastructure, civic amenities and hundreds of residences. Archaeologists traditionally assume that urbanism is accompanied by economic stratification, but, at Mohenjo-daro, qualitative evidence of inequality is absent. Drawing on early excavation data, the authors here calculate Gini coefficients of residence area, providing a quantitative proxy of economic inequality. Their results indicate that Gini coefficients, and thus inequality, declined over time, coinciding with increased prosperity and the development of the city’s street plan, indicating that governance likely helped limit economic inequality.
Keywords: South Asia, Indus Civilisation, Gini coefficient, urbanism, productivity, egalitarianism Introduction Reducing inequality is a pressing global challenge, acknowledged in the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDG-10; https://www.un.org/sustainabledevelopment/ inequality/), but the widening gap between rich and poor began long before capitalism (e.g. Kohler et al. 2017; Graeber & Wengrow 2021; Green et al. 2024). The Indus Civilisation ( c. 2600 – 1900 BC) of South Asia is an ideal case study for investigating the early dynamics of inequality. The region was home to some of the world ’s first cities (Kenoyer 1997; Possehl 1998; Menon 2008; Wright 2010; Petrie 2013;
